<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Igboland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alaigbo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Igboland is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo people</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, lying mostly east of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and extending into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> hinterland. It covers forest and savanna country and today spans Anambra, Enugu, Imo, Abia and Ebonyi states and parts of Delta and Rivers. Before colonial rule it was divided among many self-governing communities, linked by the ritual authority of Nri and the trade network of the Aro.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igboland.jpg" alt="Igboland" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NuclearVacuum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igbo_Community_in_Nigeria_and_Africa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
